<mujoco model="panda scene">
  <include file="panda.xml"/>

  <visual>
    <headlight diffuse="0.6 0.6 0.6" ambient="0.3 0.3 0.3" specular="0 0 0"/>
    <rgba haze="0.15 0.25 0.35 1"/>
    <global azimuth="120" elevation="-20"/>
  </visual>

  <asset>
    <texture type="skybox" builtin="gradient" rgb1="0.3 0.5 0.7" rgb2="0 0 0" width="512" height="3072"/>
    <texture type="2d" name="groundplane" builtin="checker" mark="edge" rgb1="0.2 0.3 0.4" rgb2="0.1 0.2 0.3"
      markrgb="0.8 0.8 0.8" width="300" height="300"/>
    <material name="groundplane" texture="groundplane" texuniform="true" texrepeat="5 5" reflectance="0.2"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <light pos="0 0 1.5" dir="0 0 -1" directional="true"/>
    <!-- The ToolHang demos' free-jointed objects (stand/frame/tool_root in
    panda.xml) carry their raw world-frame positions straight from robomimic's
    capture (see tools/robomimic_to_trajectory.py's module doc for why it
    doesn't remap them), and that source frame's Z=0 isn't this scene's floor
    height. Measured across all 200 converted demos, the lowest any object
    ever reaches is Z=-0.106 (tool/frame_root) and Z=-0.033 (stand_root); with
    the floor at the previous pos="0 0 0" default, every demo starts (and
    spends most of its time) with objects several cm *inside* the floor
    plane. Kinematic trajectory playback never noticed, since it only ever
    overwrites qpos and never resolves contacts, but the instant real physics
    steps (policy control, or anything else with the clip not actively
    driving qpos), MuJoCo's contact solver meets that penetration and throws
    the object out with a large corrective impulse.

    The floor height itself is not a free choice, though: this scene is
    composited against a real Gaussian splat table (see the scene JSON's
    "Shadow Catcher" actor, an invisible plane calibrated to the splat's real
    table surface for shadow grounding, see game_object.rs's shadow_catcher
    field doc). Given this MuJoCo actor's own placement in that scene
    (position.y = 0.88861984) versus the Shadow Catcher's (position.y =
    0.7636116), and mj_vec3's mujoco-Z-to-engine-Y mapping (mujoco.rs), the
    real table sits at mujoco-local Z = 0.7636116 - 0.88861984 = -0.125
    relative to this file's origin, not an arbitrary "clear the worst case"
    value. An earlier pass here used -0.15, which does clear -0.106 with more
    margin, but sits 2.5cm below the real table, so objects visibly sink into
    the splat once physics settles them onto it. -0.125 matches the real
    table and still clears the worst recorded penetration (-0.106) by about
    2cm; if that thinner margin ever reproduces the same explosion for a
    demo whose objects dip lower than -0.106 (or tumble further sideways
    than an unrotated pose implies), the actual mesh AABBs would need
    checking rather than pushing this number lower again. NOTE: this
    deliberately does not touch panda.xml's robot base (fixed at pos="0 0 0")
    or the trajectory data itself, since the recorded arm-to-object kinematic
    correspondence already lines up correctly using those raw coordinates;
    shifting either one to "fix" the floor height would misalign the hand's
    reach from the objects it's grasping instead. The tradeoff is a visible
    gap under the robot's base now that the floor sits below it; a pedestal
    geom filling that gap would be a reasonable follow-up if the gap looks
    wrong, but isn't needed to stop the explosion. -->
    <geom name="floor" size="0 0 0.05" type="plane" material="groundplane" pos="0 0 -0.125"/>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
